AB  - This book chapter presents a systematic review of 151 studies on assistant agents in smart classrooms, highlighting the evolution from early AI implementations to advanced applications across pedagogical support, administrative automation, collaborative learning, and accessibility and inclusion. It identifies significant technological advancements, challenges, and key issues such as ethics, bias mitigation, and real-world integration. Emphasizing the growing role of artificial intelligence (AI) and large language models (LLMs), the review underscores the necessity for ensuring their effectiveness, reliability, and ethical use. This chapter also provides a foundational resource for future research aimed at innovative AI-driven smart classrooms, integrated with digital twin technologies, to enhance educational equity and effectiveness.
