KW  - Autonomous decision-making
AB  - With the accelerated evolution of modern air combat toward high dynamics and intense antagonism, the demand for autonomous decision-making capabilities in unmanned combat aerial vehicles has become increasingly urgent. Traditional methods based on empirical rules or simplified models can hardly meet the practical requirements of future intelligent air combat. Deep reinforcement learning (DRL), which integrates deep representation learning with sequential decision-making optimization, provides a highly promising technical paradigm for autonomous decision-making in complex environments. Focusing on the application of DRL in intelligent air combat, this paper systematically sorts out four core technical challenges under high-fidelity modeling scenarios: the modeling dilemmas of high-dimensional continuous state spaces and hybrid action spaces, the learning efficiency bottleneck caused by sparse rewards, the insufficient generalization and robustness of strategies, and the predicament of large-scale multi-agent cooperative decision-making. On this basis, this paper comprehensively summarizes the representative technological advances for addressing these challenges, including hierarchical reinforcement learning and time-scale separation methods, reward shaping and prior knowledge injection techniques, robust and generalized learning mechanisms, and multi-agent reinforcement learning frameworks for cooperative combat. Through systematic comparative analysis, the applicability and limitations of various technical routes in practical air combat scenarios are clarified. Finally, from the dimensions of explainability improvement, engineering deployment, and heterogeneous swarm collaboration, future development directions are prospected, providing a clear technical roadmap for relevant research in this field.
