AB  - Medical Visual Question Answering (Med-VQA) is an emerging interdisciplinary task that integrates computer vision and natural language processing to enable automated clinical decision support based on medical images. Recent advances in deep learning have led to two prominent model paradigms: task-specific models (TSMs), which are tailored to specific datasets and clinical tasks, and large vision-language models (VLMs), which leverage large-scale pretraining to achieve strong generalization across modalities. However, a comprehensive and comparative understanding of these two approaches remains lacking. This survey presents the first systematic comparison between TSMs and VLMs in the Med-VQA domain. An unified taxonomy is introduced covering architectural strategies, knowledge integration techniques, pretraining methods, and interpretability mechanisms. Over 50 representative models are reviewed and benchmark their performance across multiple datasets. Furthermore, this paper analyses the trade-offs between accuracy, generalizability, interpretability, and computational efficiency, and examine common challenges such as hallucination, modality bias, and data scarcity. Finally, the paper outlines emerging trends and future directions, including hybrid TSM-VLM systems, instruction tuning, and trustworthy VQA frameworks, to guide the development of clinically viable Med-VQA solutions.
