AB  - Artificial intelligence and its applications have already been transformed by large language models (LLMs). This rapidly emerging field also holds immense potential to revolutionize the medical industry. This review discusses the technological frameworks, applications, challenges, and possible future directions of multimodal LLMs in healthcare diagnosis and prognosis. We emphasize how these algorithms analyze text, pictures, genetic data, and sensor inputs to enhance diagnostics, predictive analytics, and customized medicine. Transformer-based architectures, when combined with fusion methods such as joint embeddings and cross-modal attention, enable sophisticated reasoning across modalities. This feature improves the accuracy of real-time patient monitoring, cancer detection, and rare disease diagnosis. The Scopus and Google Scholar databases were used to choose the studies for this evaluation because of their extensive coverage, excellent indexed research quality, citation analysis capabilities, and sophisticated search features. Data heterogeneity, interpretability, computational cost, and ethical issues continue to be the key obstacles. The advantages of multimodal LLMs over traditional methods are illustrated through case studies. Future advancements will depend on the integration of innovative modalities such as wearable technology, IoT-based systems, and collaborative AI frameworks, along with efforts to address scalability and regulatory challenges. In order to fully realize the promise of multimodal LLMs in revolutionizing medical research and therapy, this analysis emphasizes the necessity of strong data standards, multidisciplinary collaboration, and ethical frameworks.
