AB  - Effective hate speech moderation must address explainability and bias alongside raw classification accuracy. We argue that model decisions should be accompanied by human-readable rationales—short spans of the input text that justify the assigned label—ensuring that predictions are auditable and robust. In this paper, we present Ex-HateQA, an Explainable Hate Speech Detection framework featuring Question Answering-style rationale span generation. Ex-HateQA is a parameter-efficient hybrid encoder–LLM architecture that predicts (label, rationale) tuples by formulating explanation generation as an extractive question answering (QA) task. Ex-HateQA follows the principle of parameter-efficient adaptation strategy rather than the absolute size of the underlying 7B LLM backbone. The LLM remains frozen and is updated exclusively through low-rank adapters (LoRA/QLoRA), yielding a minimal trainable parameter budget (approximately <1% of the backbone) while preserving high generation quality for extractive, copy-only rationales. The encoder pathway performs Mask-Guided Rationale Extraction (MGRE) and multi-label prediction, while the LLM pathway generates rationale spans under a strict copy-only constraint, ensuring that the output is either a verbatim substring of the post or <unknown>. It is worth mentioning that here rather than claiming an absolute theorem of causal faithfulness, Ex-HateQA is designed to encourage decision–evidence consistency: sufficiency and comprehensiveness losses regularize the encoder toward counterfactually informative rationales, and an optional alignment objective encourages agreement between MGRE token salience and the LLM’s extractive spans. We further introduce LLM-aware probes—masked prompting for counterfactual sensitivity analysis and optional rationale-tagged prompting—and optimize the model using contrastive (negative) questions to mitigate reliance on spurious triggers. Across two rigorous benchmarks, HateXplain (single-label) and CAVES (multi-label), Ex-HateQA advances the state of the art while preserving a low-latency label-screening path and a selectively invoked rationale-generation path. On HateXplain, Ex-HateQA achieves an F1 score of 0.7445 (a 7.2% improvement over the best encoder baseline) and leads span generation with a ROUGE-2 score of 0.4235 and a BLEU score of 0.5169. Furthermore, it attains the highest subgroup robustness on GMB-Sub. and GMB-BNSP, indicating a significant reduction in bias toward target communities. On CAVES, the framework reaches an F1 score of ≈0.55 (an approximate 16.8% improvement over the strongest encoder baseline) and establishes new state-of-the-art results for ROUGE-1/2/L (e.g., ROUGE-L =0.4243), while matching the highest BLEU scores. Explainability analyses corroborate these empirical gains: plausibility substantially improves (e.g., Token F1 ≈50.3, AUPRC ≈70.3), while external evaluation under an independent classifier and human judgment shows more modest but directionally consistent gains in decision–evidence consistency. We therefore interpret these results as conservative evidence of improved rationale quality rather than as a standalone proof of strong causal faithfulness. Ablation studies validate the efficacy of MGRE pre-finetuning, contrastive QA prompting, and copy-only constraints, while demonstrating robust performance in low-resource and non-English settings. Operationally, we interpret the measured label+evidence latency as an on-demand explanation budget within a cascaded human-in-the-loop moderation pipeline, rather than as the cost of synchronous explanation generation for every incoming post at ingestion scale. We therefore position Ex-HateQA as a framework with deterministic extractive grounding, empirically supported faithfulness-oriented behavior, and a deployment scope centered on cascaded moderation workflows rather than universal firehose-scale full-explanation serving. Warning: This paper contains samples that may be upsetting.
