AB  - Many children on the autism spectrum use forms of language, such as echolalia and scripting, that are more imitative than self-generated. Nonetheless, these unconventional forms of language can have semantic meaning and/or pragmatic intent and thus are best viewed as attempts to communicate and relate. Two paradigms will be used to discuss scripting and its role in language and communication development and use. A developmental language model and a linguistic-conversational analysis will serve as the perspectives from which scripting will be addressed. When scripting is embedded in these paradigms, intervention takes a well-defined direction. The dynamic role of scripts in the language acquisition process and in communication interactions will be considered from three reference points: why children use scripts, how the child’s communicative partners can respond to scripts, and how parents and clinicians can facilitate the child’s use of more conventional forms of language.
