AB  - Setting the stage for an in-depth exploration of the leading transformative technologies reshaping urban ecological sustainability, this introductory chapter illuminates the evolutionary dynamics, highlights the key computational advancements, identifies the emerging synergistic opportunities, maps the evolving applications, and addresses the implementation challenges, all arising from the adoption and convergence of Generative AI (GenAI) and Quantum Computing (QC) in smarter eco-cities. After presenting the background and context of the book, it situates this innovative paradigm within the trajectory of urban development, tracing the progression from eco-cities and smart cities to smart eco-cities and ultimately smarter eco-cities—which serve as living laboratories, experimental sites, and integrative frameworks for advancing environmental sustainability, climate resilience, and infrastructure optimization in complex urban environments. Based on these insights, it introduces a comprehensive framework for integrating the urban, ecological, technological, and computational paradigms that underpin generative, hybrid, and foundation modeling for smarter eco-city intelligence. Building on this integrated perspective, it then examines how AI, GenAI, and QC interact synergistically to establish the computational, algorithmic, and systemic foundations necessary for next-generation smarter eco-city intelligence. Thereafter, it details Quantum Deep Generative Models (QDGMs)—including Quantum Generative Adversarial Networks (QGANs), Quantum Variational Autoencoders (QVAEs), Quantum Diffusion Models (QDMs), Quantum Transformers and Large Language Models (LLMs), as well as the underlying quantum principles, architectural mechanisms, and specialized algorithmic strategies. Subsequently, a comparative analysis assesses how these generative architectures incorporate and operationalize these core quantum foundations, drawing on evidence synthesized from more than 125 empirical and theoretical studies. Emerging applications demonstrate their potential to enhance urban–environmental intelligence, addressing energy, infrastructure, environmental, and climate challenges. The chapter further evaluates the environmental risks, energy implications, and deployment constraints of QDGMs, offering a multidimensional perspective on their technical, computational, hardware, operational, ethical, and governance challenges. Insights from these analyses are synthesized to present a coherent overview of opportunities, limitations, and pathways for integrating quantum generative intelligence into smarter eco-city ecosystems. Through this synthesis, the chapter establishes a unified conceptual and empirical foundation for a detailed exploration in the subsequent chapters, which delve into the architectural design, empirical performance, technical feasibility, contextual relevance, and practical implementation of quantum generative urban-environmental intelligence. Ultimately, it underscores the interdisciplinary, transdisciplinary, and experimental nature of smarter eco-cities and positions this intelligence as a next-generation paradigm for delivering systemic, scalable, and adaptive solutions to the complex challenges of contemporary urban ecosystems.
