KW  - Multi-scale semantic entropy
KW  - Hallucination early warning
KW  - Adaptive threshold
KW  - Semantic entropy gradient
KW  - Hierarchical uncertainty quantification
KW  - Explainable AI
AB  - Hallucinations pose a critical challenge for Large Language Models (LLMs) in high-stakes applications. We introduce the Multi-scale Adaptive Semantic Entropy (MASE) framework, which hierarchically computes semantic uncertainty across tokens, phrases, sentences, and paragraphs for precise detection and early intervention. Central to MASE is the novel Semantic Entropy Gradient (SEG), modeling uncertainty evolution dynamically during generation, complemented by a context-aware adaptive threshold that adjusts sensitivity to tasks and domains. Extensive experiments on six benchmarks (TruthfulQA with 817 examples, HaluEval with 35,000, FACTOR with 4,030, BEGIN with 6,468, SelfCheckGPT with 1,908, FActScore with 500; totaling over 48,000 examples)—including detailed ablations and comparisons—validate MASE’s superiority, achieving 70% cost reduction over traditional semantic entropy while detecting 73% of hallucinations within 5 tokens (approximately 30% of typical generation length), outperforming baselines by an average of 9.6% in F1-score. An integrated visual module enables exact localization of hallucination positions and types, offering practical guidance for model refinement.
