AB  - The complexity and dynamic nature of unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) swarm networks (USNs) require intelligent, scalable, and adaptive solutions for full autonomy. Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) models such as generative adversarial networks, variational autoencoders, diffusion models, and transformers have emerged as powerful tools for addressing optimization challenges in USNs. This article makes two complementary contributions. First, this paper reviews GenAI models, detailing their architecture, strengths, limitations, and potential to enhance autonomy in USNs. We highlight how GenAI, particularly when combined with multi-agent deep reinforcement learning (MA-DRL), supports adaptive state estimation, synthetic data augmentation, knowledge transfer, and intent-aware decision-making across key USN applications, including task allocation, trajectory optimization, channel estimation, beamforming, resource management, energy harvesting, and security. A detailed survey of recent GenAI-integrated value and policy-based MA-DRL frameworks is provided, with a focus on modifications in internal neural networks, training, loss, and reward function design. Second, beyond the survey, an illustrative case study is presented featuring a large language model-driven heterogeneous multi-agent self- and cross-attention-based multi-expert actor-critic framework for a high-altitude platform-assisted USN mission, jointly optimizing UAV trajectory, task offloading, and resource allocation under complex mobility, quality of service, and resource constraints. Simulation results show that the proposed framework reduces the age of task processing by approximately 21–48% and normalized energy consumption by 19–44% compared with the considered benchmarks. Finally, the paper identifies practical deployment issues and open research challenges for advancing GenAI-enabled autonomy in USNs.
