AB  - Chapter 7 surveys data-driven epidemic modeling as an alternative and complement to mechanistic approaches, focusing on methods that learn predictive structure directly from surveillance data. It begins with classical time-series formulations, including observation models for counts, ARIMA/SARIMA, state-space methods, and nowcasting layers that correct reporting delays and stabilize the effective forecasting boundary. The chapter then moves to machine-learning models that use engineered lagged and exogenous features, followed by deep-learning architectures that learn representations from sequential, multimodal, and spatio-temporal inputs. It concludes with large language models, presented not primarily as replacements for epidemiological forecasters, but as tools for extracting, structuring, and integrating textual information such as outbreak bulletins and policy reports into forecasting pipelines. Throughout, the chapter stresses leakage-safe design, time-respecting validation, uncertainty assessment, and robustness under distribution shift.
