AB  - Shipbuilding welding quality fundamentally dictates the structural integrity and safety of marine vessels. However, these quality problems are triggered by multidimensional, interacting risk factors, which traditional reliability assessments struggle to interpret due to a lack of semantic depth. Therefore, this paper proposes a coupling analysis framework integrating a knowledge graph and the NK model to quantify the coupling intensity among factors of ship welding quality problems and reveal their semantic relationships. Specifically, a domain ontology is constructed based on Grounded Theory and combined with a large language model (LLM) to achieve automated construction of the knowledge graph, providing semantic constraints. By combining mutual information with directed transition probability, a G-NK (Graph-enhanced NK) integrated coupling model is constructed to measure semantic coupling among multidimensional factors and mine potential coupling patterns. Using welding quality problem reports from Z Shipbuilding Company and an external validation dataset from another shipyard, the framework is evaluated in terms of ranking quality, coupling-strength discrimination, interpretability, expert consistency, and robustness. Results show that G-NK outperforms complex-network and Bayesian forest baselines, achieving an nDCG@15 of 0.705. Compared with the traditional NK model, it produces more discriminative coupling-strength results and transforms vague high-coupling factor sets into fine-grained risk propagation paths. Informativeness evaluation further shows that G-NK explanatory paths preserve more model-output information than NK-only and KG-only baselines. The model rankings are also highly consistent with human and LLM expert judgments. This approach equips marine production teams with actionable insights for detecting latent problems and designing targeted interventions. The source code is available at https://github.com/sakura1234876/marine-welding-gnk-risk-analysis.
