AB  - The increasing scale, heterogeneity, and multimodality of urban–environmental data have exposed the limitations of task-specific and domain-bounded modeling approaches. Generative AI, particularly Deep Generative Models (DGMs) and Foundation Models (FMs), offers significant opportunities to advance complex urban–environmental systems. While prior research has examined DGMs and their hybrid and mixed variants, the integration and application of pretrained, adapted, and multimodal FMs in urban–environmental intelligence remain underexplored. Moreover, urban and environmental FMs are often studied as separate strands, providing little synthesis of their architectural convergence, underlying model classes, cross-domain interoperability, multimodal fusion strategies, or capacity to represent coupled urban–ecological dynamics in smarter eco-cities. This study addresses this gap by positioning key FM categories as integrative and system-level enablers of next-generation urban–environmental intelligence for smarter eco-cities. It synthesizes the theoretical, architectural, functional, and practical dimensions of geospatial, language, vision, and timeseries FMs and their multimodal subsets, as well as examines how they extend the capacities of DGMs and their hybrid and mixed variants. In doing so, it establishes a coherent framework for scalable, adaptive, and multimodal intelligence capable of addressing the growing complexity of urban ecological sustainability challenges. The synthesis demonstrates that FMs enable large-scale pretrained knowledge representations that support transfer learning, rapid adaptation, and holistic reasoning across geospatial, textual, visual, and semantic domains. Multimodal and geospatial FMs show strong potential for automated infrastructure mapping, land-use classification, environmental monitoring, and disaster assessment using heterogeneous satellite, aerial, and street-level data. Geospatially grounded language models enhance spatial reasoning, geo-entity understanding, and location-based decision support. Vision FMs achieve state-of-the-art performance in detection, segmentation, and classification tasks, efficiently extracting spatial and environmental patterns from large-scale imagery. Vision–language and multimodal models further improve scene understanding, semantic alignment, and zero-shot and few-shot generalization, strengthening urban sensing, navigation, and environmental monitoring capabilities. Cross-architectural integration with DGMs and hybrid and mixed models enables adaptive, scalable, and context-aware intelligence across urban–environmental tasks, helping to address data scarcity, heterogeneity, and operational complexity. The findings highlight the strategic value of combining FMs with deep generative, hybrid, and mixed architectures to support large-scale, interoperable, evidence-informed decision-making for smarter eco-city development. This synthesis provides actionable guidance for strategic planning, infrastructure management, environmental monitoring, and climate resilience, while advancing the understanding of how FMs can bridge urban dynamics and environmental processes.
