UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0950705126003795
KW  - Knowledge-driven reasoning systems
KW  - Cognitive reasoning and decision-making
KW  - Multi-agent intelligent systems
KW  - Semantic and pragmatic knowledge modeling
KW  - Context-aware language understanding
KW  - Cyberbullying detection and mitigation
AB  - Modern toxic communication increasingly manifests through implicit aggression, sarcasm-laden discourse, contextual insults, and cognitively masked hostility, rendering traditional keyword-centric and surface-level detection paradigms inadequate for reliable interpretation and effective intervention. Although recent advances in artificial intelligence and generative language models have improved detection capabilities, existing approaches remain fragmented and lack a unified, knowledge-driven reasoning architecture capable of jointly modeling semantic meaning, pragmatic intent, and cognitive context while performing detection and mitigation within a single end-to-end framework. To address this limitation, we propose RaSA-BoDX, a novel multi-layer, meta-cognitive, and agentic reasoning framework that formulates cyberbullying analysis as a knowledge-constrained reasoning problem rather than a purely statistical classification task. RaSA-BoDX integrates tone-intent grounding, sarcasm and ambiguity reasoning, emotional anchoring, and multi-turn conversational modeling into a dynamic cognitive analysis pipeline. The framework employs CrewAI-orchestrated multi-agent collaboration, Model Context Protocol (MCP)-based knowledge validation, cognitive contradiction reasoning, and multi-LLM swarm consensus to enable adaptive, explainable, and context-aware decision-making. A key contribution of RaSA-BoDX is its meaning-fidelity-preserving mitigation mechanism, which reconceptualizes mitigation as a cognitively constrained transformation instead of superficial paraphrasing. This mechanism ensures the preservation of original communicative intent, emotional coherence, and pragmatic context through EmoShift anchoring, iterative chain-of-thought stabilization, semantic consistency locking, and dual-agent verification. Experimental results across multiple benchmark datasets demonstrate that RaSA-BoDX achieves robust detection performance while delivering reliable, context-preserving mitigation under adversarial and ambiguous conditions. To the best of our knowledge, this work represents the first knowledge-driven, cognitive-level, multi-agent, multi-LLM framework that unifies cyberbullying detection and mitigation through explicit reasoning processes, establishing a new paradigm for intelligent, robust, and human-aligned cyber safety systems.
